  "caption": "Each point is a study: its estimate across, its standard error down, so larger studies are at the top. The triangle is where 95% of studies would fall around the common-effect estimate if there were no bias and no heterogeneity. Asymmetry does not by itself show non-reporting bias (such as publication bias): differences between studies, bias in smaller studies and chance can all cause it. For odds ratios, the estimate and its standard error are related, which can make a funnel look asymmetric by itself. No test for asymmetry is run here.",
